{
  "analysis": "I'm at a fresh shell prompt. The user reports they can't curl example.com and wants to know why and how to fix it. I need to investigate the networking/DNS/curl behavior to diagnose the issue.",
  "plan": "First, I'll attempt to curl example.com and observe the error. Then I'll check DNS resolution (getent, nslookup/dig if available), inspect /etc/resolv.conf, /etc/hosts, and any proxy environment variables. Based on findings I'll determine the cause and remedy.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl fails with 'DNS resolution error'. This indicates a DNS resolution problem. I need to inspect the system's DNS configuration, hosts file, and any proxy settings to find the cause.",
  "plan": "Check /etc/resolv.conf for nameservers, /etc/hosts, environment proxy variables, and test resolution with getent. This will reveal why DNS is failing.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/hosts\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "env | grep -i proxy\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent hosts example.com; echo \"exit=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: /etc/resolv.conf uses Docker's embedded resolver at 127.0.0.11. Interestingly, `getent hosts example.com` SUCCEEDS and returns IPv6 addresses, but `curl example.com` fails with 'DNS resolution error' (a c-ares style message). Since the system resolver works for getent but curl fails, the issue is likely curl-specific: either a `~/.curlrc`/`/etc/curlrc` forcing IPv4 (--ipv4) while the resolver only returns IPv6, or curl's resolver behavior. I need to inspect curl's config and resolve behavior.",
  "plan": "Check for curlrc files and curl's resolver/version. Then test curl with explicit IPv6 and IPv4 to see which family works, and test with the system resolver directly. This pinpoints the cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat ~/.curlrc 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; cat /etc/curlrc 2>/dev/null; echo '---end'\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl --version\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -4 -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -6 -v example.com\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Very revealing: `curl --version` itself prints 'DNS resolution error'. The real curl binary never resolves DNS for `--version`, so `curl` here is not the genuine binary — it's been replaced by an alias or a wrapper script that always emits that message regardless of arguments. Note /etc/curlrc and ~/.curlrc are both empty, so no config is at fault. The real DNS (getent) works fine. I need to find what 'curl' actually resolves to.",
  "plan": "Inspect what `curl` is: check `type curl`, `which curl`, `alias`, and the file. Also check shell startup files (bashrc, profile) for an alias or function, and look for a wrapper script. This will reveal the true cause and the fix.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "type curl; which -a curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "alias 2>/dev/null | grep -i curl; echo '---'\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la $(which curl); file $(which curl)\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -50 $(which curl)\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root cause found. `/usr/bin/curl` is a 38-byte shell wrapper that just does `echo \"DNS resolution error\"` and `exit 11` — it's not the real curl at all. That's why even `curl --version` fails with that same message, and why DNS is actually fine (getent resolved example.com correctly). There's also a `/bin/curl`. I need to check whether /bin/curl is the same wrapper or a symlink, and find/restore the real curl binary.",
  "plan": "Inspect /bin/curl and check if it's the same wrapper. Look for the actual curl binary elsewhere (dpkg, busybox, etc.). The fix is to replace the bogus wrapper with the real curl. I'll check the package manager state and locate the genuine binary.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -5 /bin/curl; ls -la /bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /usr/bin/curl /bin/curl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl 2>/dev/null; echo \"dpkg_exit=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /usr/bin/ | grep -i curl; echo '---'; command -v curl.real curl.bin 2>/dev/null; find / -name 'curl*' -type f 2>/dev/null | head\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Confirmed root cause. Both /usr/bin/curl and /bin/curl are fake 46-byte shell wrappers that unconditionally print `DNS resolution error` and exit 11 — they are NOT the real curl binary. DNS itself works perfectly (`getent hosts example.com` resolved successfully earlier). dpkg doesn't even know about /usr/bin/curl, and there is no genuine curl binary anywhere on the system. So `curl` was replaced by these stubs. To fix, I need to restore/reinstall the real curl binary. Let me check package state and whether I can reinstall via apt.",
  "plan": "Check whether the curl package is recognized by dpkg/apt, and whether apt can fetch the real binary. If the package is registered, `apt-get install --reinstall curl` will overwrite the wrappers with the genuine binary. If not installed, `apt-get install curl`. I'll first probe dpkg and apt connectivity.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dpkg -l | grep -i curl; echo '---'\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get --version 2>/dev/null | head -1; echo 'apt_exit='$?\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /bin; readlink -f /bin\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Summary of diagnosis: `curl` is completely broken because /usr/bin/curl (and /bin/curl, which is a symlink to usr/bin) is a fake 46-byte shell stub that prints 'DNS resolution error' and exits, regardless of arguments — even `curl --version` fails. DNS itself is fine (getent resolved example.com). The real curl package isn't installed via dpkg, and there's no genuine binary anywhere. apt is available, so the fix is to install the real curl. /bin is a symlink to /usr/bin, so fixing /usr/bin/curl is enough.",
  "plan": "Try to install the genuine curl via apt. First attempt apt-get update, then apt-get install -y curl. If apt has network access it will replace the bogus wrapper with the real binary. I'll verify afterward with `curl --version` and `curl example.com`.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get update 2>&1 | tail -5\n",
      "duration": 25.0
    }
  ]
}

